.TH WLAN 3
.SH NAME
wlan \- the radio of an 802.11 interface driven by a program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #ω /dev
.PP
.BI /dev/wlan N /data
.BI /dev/wlan N /ctl
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An ethernet interface of type
.B wlan
.RB ( ether\fIN\fP=type=wlan
in
.IR plan9.ini (8))
is an 802.11 interface as
.IR ether (3)'s
WLAN drivers are: the kernel's 802.11 code scans, authenticates,
associates and ciphers
.RB ( essid ,
.B bssid
and keys by its
.B clone
file;
.IR wpa (8)
for WPA), but its radio is a program, a user-level driver of the
hardware or a simulation.
The program has the interface's directory
.BI wlan N
here, after its
.IR ether (3)
number
.IR N .
.PP
Read, the data file gives a frame the interface sends, or a request
to tune (no frame), each read one, after a header of 12 bytes:
the channel (2 bytes, little endian), the rate (in 500 kb/s), flags
(1: tune only, no frame), the association id (2 bytes) and the
access point's address (6 bytes): the channel and the access point
the radio is to be on.
Written, it takes a frame received, after a header of 12 bytes: the
channel (2 bytes), the signal strength (in dBm, signed), flags (1:
the next 8 bytes are the frame's timestamp, little endian) and those
8 bytes.
The data file is open once, by the radio; frames sent while it is
not are dropped.
.PP
The kernel's 802.11 code scans the 2.4 GHz channels 1 to 13 and the
5 GHz channels 36 to 64, 100 to 144 and 149 to 165 (20 MHz), the
common ones 1, 6 and 11 first and 36 to 48 and 149 to 165 between the
2.4 GHz ones; a radio ignores what it cannot tune to.
On the channels with radar detection (DFS, 52 to 144) it only listens
for beacons, 200 ms, and sends nothing until it has heard the access
point it wants; on the others it sends probe requests (when an
.B essid
is set) and waits 200 ms (2.4 GHz) or 100 ms (5 GHz).
There is no regulatory domain: it scans every channel of the list.
The rate in the header is 802.11b's or OFDM's (6 to 54 Mb/s; on 5 GHz
only these): for management and group addressed frames the access
point's lowest, for data the kernel's choice; while scanning 1 Mb/s
on 2.4 GHz and 6 Mb/s on 5 GHz.
The channel of a frame received is not used: an access point's is the
one its beacons name (the DS parameter set, else on 5 GHz the HT
operation element's primary channel), else the one the radio was tuned
to.
.PP
Written,
.B ctl
takes
.BI ea " addr"
(12 hex digits): the station's address, set by the radio from its
hardware before the interface is used, and
.BI caps " hex"
: the radio's capability elements (id, length, contents, one after
another: HT capabilities, 45; VHT, 191; HE, 255 with 35), which the
association request carries where the access point's beacon offers the
same.
.B "irq on
(and
.BR off )
hands the frames received to wifi.c from a timer function, at interrupt
level, as a real radio's driver does from its interrupt handler, instead
of from the writing process: a test of what wifi.c does there
.RI ( sys/src/build/host/tests/wlan ).
With an access point that offers WMM the association request has WMM's
information element and the station's data frames are QoS data (TID 0);
the interface's
.B ifstats
says what the access point offers
.RB ( offers: " wmm ht vht he" )
and whether the data is QoS
.RB ( qos: " wmm" ).
The radio sets the access point's station up from the association
response it passes on (its HT capabilities, its WMM parameters).
.PP
The receive header's signal (dBm; 0: the radio has none) chooses the
node: where the radio gives it, wifi.c joins only after its scan went
once over the channels since it heard the first node that matches, and
then the strongest of those it heard in the last 5 s (a mesh's access
point rather than a far repeater), as OpenBSD chooses: the strongest on
5 GHz if its signal is at least \-70 dBm, even where one on 2.4 GHz is
stronger, else the strongest on either band.
The interface's
.B ifstats
says the joined node's signal
.RB ( signal: )
and each node's in its
.B node:
line, after the channel (dBm, 0: none); 9front's line has no such
column.
There is no roaming yet: associated, the radio hears only its channel.
.PP
The radio answers aggregates (A-MPDU) with block acks: wifi.c agrees to
the access point's block ack agreements (buffer size at most 64, no
A-MSDU in them), holds the frames of such a TID until they are in order
(at most 100 ms for one missing), asks for one itself for TID 0, and
takes A-MSDUs apart; the radio learns of the agreements from the ADDBA
and DELBA frames passing through it.  Only QoS data with data, to this
station, from the access point, acknowledged normally or by block ack
and not fragmented is reordered (as OpenBSD's net80211); frames behind
the window are dropped, and a frame more than a window past its end
moves it only when eight such come one after the other (OpenBSD's
ba_winmiss): a single wild sequence number no longer runs the window
away (the ThinkBook's rtw89, 2026-11-19, whose WLAN then carried nothing).
.B ifstats
counts the frames held, the waits given up, those dropped behind the
window and past it and the window's jumps, with its head
.RB ( rxba: ),
the TIDs we aggregate on
.RB ( txba: )
and CCMP's replays.
Read, it gives the state, the capabilities and counts of frames.
Only the host owner opens the files.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/etherwlan.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/port/devwlan.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ether (3),
.IR wpa (8),
.IR plan9.ini (8)
.SH BUGS
No 6 GHz channels: their numbers are those of the 2.4 GHz band; the
header would need the band.
HT at 20 or 40 MHz (where the access point's HT operation names a
secondary channel), VHT at up to 80 MHz on 5 GHz (where its VHT
operation says 80), no HE and no 160 MHz yet: the radio's firmware
(rtw89) chooses the rates.
.br
Written for 9space.
